A||||12||firstfiero||question about clearcoat?||||14||
Z||000000||firstfiero||08-30-2001||11:00 AM||||    Well I want to get a cheap paint job at Macco and it's going to cost me 700 to 800 because of the clear coat on the back needs sanded off. I think I might do it myself. Is this difficult and what is the best way to go about it. I can scratch it off with my fingernail so It can't be that tough.<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000001||Joe Torma||08-30-2001||11:31 AM||||Prep it yourself and save some money!  Sanding by hand is ok, if you have lots of time on your hands.  Otherwise if you don't have air tools, get an inexpensive electric orbital sander(like a DeWalt or something) and sand with 180-220paper  You'll want to get all of that clear off.||||reg||1
Z||000002||firstfiero||08-30-2001||11:37 AM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.imagestation.com/picture/sraid26/p3fe0910cd58329fb2da43e7ae19e18df/fe43210d.jpg">  <P>here is a pic...sorry it's raining and that kinda covers it up.<P>------------------<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by firstfiero (edited 08-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||08-30-2001||12:00 PM||||Or you can just hand sand it off, wet or dry with #400 sandpaper. Knowing that MAACO dont prime anything except bare filler or metal, Id put a coat of sandable primer over it when your done. Dont use the cheap enamel primer, make sure it says 'sandable' or 'Laquer Primer/Surfacer'||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||08-30-2001||12:02 PM||||Dewd I recomend not using MACCO. Even if you prep the car yourself their painters are not top notch. If you get their top notch paint job all your getting is better quality paint and in most cases not even that. <BR>I worked in a MACCO and I can tell you their work is shoddy at best in the 1 or 2 months I was there we had 4 cars return with peeling paint||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||08-30-2001||01:50 PM||||I just had my paint done there. Looks great, even if I did have to remove a little overspray from a few annoying places.<BR>First, Maaco WILL put a sealer-primer on. The paint will NOT hold otherwise.<BR>Second, the paint they use is just fine. It's the prep-work that will fail, not the paint.<P>Do the sanding yourself. I'd recommend 320grit WET sandpaper. Slap it on a small sanding block (5"?) and work in circles until the clearcoat is OFF. Then switch to a 400grit to finish up. I wouldn't primer it at all unless you have a spray-gun. Even then, it'd be better to let them do it. They've got a clean spraybooth. Dirt in your primer REALLY sucks.<P>Now, having said that, I feel I got a great deal from $240. However, I did not expect a PRO job, and neither did I get one. The masking they did was shabby, and I am glad that I'm removing my trim to repaint myself. Otherwise, though, the pain looks great, and with the exception of a few stone chips I picked up in the mountains (   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> ) is flawless. <BR>Go for it, at least until you can afford a better job done.<P>I have to post pictures of my new(ly painted) car...<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 08-30-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000006||firstfiero||08-30-2001||02:20 PM||||     What about just spraying over it. It would look a hell of alot better then it does now! Anyway I agree with the last post....I'm not looking for a show car job...I just want to be able to look at my car and not see the white on the back deck.<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||fieronv||08-30-2001||02:30 PM||||DON'T just spray over the old paint!!! unless you want to have a paint job that only last a month or so and looks like sh**. You have to have a surface that the paint will stick to and it has to be smooth. The difference between a good and great paint job is surface prep.<P>------------------<BR>85GT (twoloud)<p>[This message has been edited by fieronv (edited 08-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||08-30-2001||02:35 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> Spraying over it?!???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P>NNooooooooooooooooooooooooooooooooooooo!<P><BR>Don't do that. It'll peel off within a few weeks, and instead of dandruff, your car will have scabies!<P>Do the prep RIGHT, and the paint will look GREAT. ||||reg||11
Z||000009||87GTBro||08-30-2001||03:19 PM||||When we painted my brother's GT, we did it the RIGHT way.  Now, you probably don't have the time or patience to do this, but we did an off-body paint job ourselves, from beginning to end.  <P>We removed all the body panels except for the roof skin and we sanded them down to the fiberglass in some spots.  We started with 220 wet sanding and slowly worked our way up on the grit to 400, 600, and finally finished sanding with 1200.  (All wet sanding)<P>Now most people probably wouldn't have the patience or the $ to do this, but we built our own spray booth in the garage out of 1x2's and thick painters plastic.  We bought a little box fan and a couple A/C air filters so we can have some ventilation without introducing contaminants into the air.  (Sucks to have bugs land on your car while the paint is wet.)<P>First we laid a primer coat on the car, then three coats of color and 4 coats of clear.<P>Some of you probably know which car I am talking about...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/gallery/Cars/Sand31.jpg"> <BR>A good paint job definitely makes a difference.  I wouldn't suggest doing what we did (because it took so long and it was a lot of work) but I would suggest asking around and find out who does the best paint jobs in your area and pay a little extra to get it done right.<P>M2C<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87GTBrosig_3.gif">  <BR>AOL IM: GTFiero86Dan<BR>'86 GT 4 speed<BR>'87 GT Auto||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||08-30-2001||03:39 PM||||I would agree with 87GTBro. However, some don't have the time to do this. Maaco is a bandaid. My paint probably won't last for more than 2 years. But then, I don't want it to. I will give it a nicer paint job, frame-off when I have the money and time. For now, though, it looks great, and kicks the CRAP out of having a Fiero with Melanoma. <P>On a side note: How the HELL did you get the primer to adhere to bare fiberglass sanded with 1200 grit!??! I wouldn't go smoother than 400... There's nothing for the paint to bite!||||reg||7
Z||000011||rogergarrison||08-30-2001||04:48 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B><BR>First, Maaco WILL put a sealer</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They do put sealer on, but sealer and primer are 2 different things. Primer will make the edges where you feather the clear blend in smoothly and not show in final finish. sealer just makes for a uniform color under the color coat, and helps it to bond a little better in areas not sanded too well. I never use sealer myself, primer is far better, and to get an even color, i put a coat of a mixed up base close to it under the final color coats. Lisas was gold and i mixed a base of dark blue left overs for first coat, then 3 coats of her color over it. the sealer they use is just sprayed like a coat of paint. Primer has to be sanded again before its painted. I get all kinds of dirt and crap in primer, dont matter since its sanded out anyway. Dont count on primer from MAACO, one here dont even have any, they just use the sealer instead and you cant sand the sealer, its enamel based.<P><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 08-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mach10||08-30-2001||04:57 PM||||Ah. I was under the impression that it was a sealer-primer they used. That is very possible. Regardless, the paint is nice and uniform, and holding nicely to the old base-coat. Of course, I spent a lot of time sanding, so some of the credit goes to yours truly   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I see what you mean, though. I sanded the bad areas almost completely. There was almost no topcoat left. My decklid (where the clearcoat had gone) was almost all down to the original primer. Took forever to make it nice and smooth. Of course, a large sanding block helps... For the rest of the car, I just scuffed the clearcoat down with 320, washed, scuffed again, washed, then moved up to a 400. The end result was nice. There's no ridges at all on the car (except where I had forgotten to fill a gouge.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>I will concede that you can do a lot better than Maaco. However, you can spend 200 on a detailing and have the car looking not even half as good. Just don't let 'em do the jams, and the car will shine like a new button. Just be sure to take your time sanding, and do a thorough job of it. <P>I really should post some pics...<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 08-30-2001).]||||reg||7
